88299710676e-05,-0.13761894404888153,-2.3841855067985307e-07,-0.0002079985715681687,-6.437280717364047e-06],"request":{"active_prefix":"","input_ids":[151644,8948,198,2610,525,1207,16948,11,3465,553,54364,14817,13,1446,525,264,10950,17847,13,151645,198,151644,872,198,50,3948,279,3491,3019,553,3019,13,20678,697,1590,4226,304,1124,79075,90,1112,92,382,32,1555,702,30232,400,30529,37018,90,22,15170,19,92,2418,220,15920,315,279,2701,22879,525,3204,5106,22879,369,279,1555,1939,58,6405,921,810,1722,445,309,3563,587,3071,25643,551,7,16,9961,626,12670,856,284,320,18,11,15,701,379,284,320,15,11,17,626,1502,31732,32,8,57960,7265,90,5187,2555,92,220,19,24984,220,22,1124,408,90,5187,2555,31716,497,379,317,1502,31732,33,8,57960,7265,90,5187,2555,92,220,22,24984,220,19,1124,408,90,5187,2555,31716,497,856,488,379,317,1502,31732,34,8,57960,7265,90,5187,2555,92,481,19,14,22,24984,220,16,1124,408,90,5187,2555,31716,497,220,17,18481,488,379,317,1502,31732,35,8,57960,7265,90,5187,2555,92,220,16,24984,220,22,14,19,1124,408,90,5187,2555,31716,497,220,18,18481,488,379,317,1502,31732,36,8,57960,7265,90,5187,2555,92,220,16,19,24984,481,23,1124,408,90,5187,2555,31716,497,320,15,11,15,1106,1502,31732,37,8,57960,7265,90,5187,2555,92,481,16,17,24984,220,17,16,1124,408,90,5187,2555,31716,497,856,317,1502,31732,38,8,57960,7265,90,5187,2555,92,481,17,24984,481,22,14,17,1124,408,90,5187,2555,31716,497,220,17,18481,317,1502,31732,39,8,57960,7265,90,5187,2555,92,481,16,14,18,24984,220,22,14,16,17,1124,408,90,5187,2555,31716,497,220,18,18481,317,24157,6405,2533,6269,279,11931,315,279,4396,2606,11,18663,553,76602,13,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nA line has slope $-\\frac{7}{4}.$  Which of the following vectors are possible direction vectors for the line?\n\n[asy]\nusepackage(\"amsmath\");\n\nunitsize(1 cm);\n\npair x = (3,0), y = (0,2);\n\nlabel(\"(A) $\\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 7 \\end{pmatrix}$\", y);\nlabel(\"(B) $\\begin{pmatrix} 7 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix}$\", x + y);\nlabel(\"(C) $\\begin{pmatrix} -4/7 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}$\", 2*x + y);\nlabel(\"(D) $\\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 7/4 \\end{pmatrix}$\", 3*x + y);\nlabel(\"(E) $\\begin{pmatrix} 14 \\\\ -8 \\end{pmatrix}$\", (0,0));\nlabel(\"(F) $\\begin{pmatrix} -12 \\\\ 21 \\end{pmatrix}$\", x);\nlabel(\"(G) $\\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ -7/2 \\end{pmatrix}$\", 2*x);\nlabel(\"(H) $\\begin{pmatrix} -1/3 \\\\ 7/12 \\end{pmatrix}$\", 3*x);\n[/asy]\n\nEnter the letters of the correct options, separated by commas.","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"20f5d0c1a204343230e3823f9ac963fcde14d1fdffca5dbb0fd56e2fb2aaca09"},"seconds":45.63598680496216,"seed":375918380648338472,"stop":"eos","text":"To determine which of the given vectors can serve as direction vectors for a line with slope \\(-\\frac{7}{4}\\), we need to recall the relationship between the slope of a line and its direction vector. For a line with slope \\(m = -\\frac{7}{4}\\), a direction vector \\(\\begin{pmatrix} a \\\\ b \\end{pmatrix}\\) must satisfy the condition \\(\\frac{b}{a} = -\\frac{7}{4}\\).\n\nLet's check each vector:\n\n- **(A) \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\\\ 7 \\end{pmatrix}\\)**: The slope would be \\(\\frac{7}{4}\\), which is not \\(-\\frac{7}{4}\\). So, this is not a possible direction vector.\n- **(B) \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 7 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix}\\)**: The slope would be \\(\\frac{4}{7}\\), which is not \\(-\\frac{7}{4}\\). So, this is not a possible direction vector.\n- **(C) \\(\\begin{pmatrix} -\\frac{4}{7} \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}\\)**: The slope would be \\(\\frac{1}{-\\frac{4}{7}} = -\\frac{7}{4}\\), which matches the slope of the line. So, this is a possible direction vector.\n- **(D) \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ \\frac{7}{4} \\end{pmatrix}\\)**: The slope would be \\(\\frac{\\frac{7}{4}}{1} = \\frac{7}{4}\\), which is not \\(-\\frac{7}{4}\\). So, this is not a possible direction vector.\n- **(E) \\(\\begin{pmatrix} 14 \\\\ -8 \\end{pmatrix}\\)**: The slope would be \\(\\frac{-8}{14} = -\\frac{4}{7}\\), which is not \\(-\\frac{7}{4}\\). So, this is not a possible direction vector.\n- **(F) \\(\\begin{pmatrix} -12 \\\\ 21 \\end{pmatrix}\\)**: The slope would be \\(\\frac{21}{-12} = -\\frac{7}{4}\\), which matches the slope of the line. So, this is a possible direction vector.\n- **(G) \\(\\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\\\ -\\frac{7}{2} \\end{pmatrix}\\)**: The slope would be \\(\\frac{-\\frac{7}{2}}{-2} = \\frac{7}{4}\\), which is not \\(-\\frac{7}{4}\\). So, this is not a possible direction vector.\n- **(H) \\(\\begin{pmatrix} -\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{7}{12} \\end{pmatrix}\\)**: The slope would be \\(\\frac{\\frac{7}{12}}{-\\frac{1}{3}} = -\\frac{7}{4}\\), which matches the slope of the line. So, this is a possible direction vector.\n\nThus, the correct options are (C), (F), and (H).\n\n\\boxed{C,F,H}"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{C,F,H}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true}}
